„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd he said unto them, Unto you it is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of God: but unto them that are without, all these things are done in parables:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ­that seeing they may see, and not perceive; and hearing they may hear, and not understand; lest at any time they should be converted, and their sins should be forgiven them.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses˜„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙXAnd he said unto them, Know ye not this parable? and how then will ye know all parables?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textºThe sower soweth the word.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§And these are they by the way side, where the word is sown; but when they have heard, Satan cometh immediately, and taketh away the word that was sown in their hearts.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And these are they likewise which are sown on stony ground; who, when they have heard the word, immediately receive it with gladness;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£and have no root in themselves, and so endure but for a time: afterward, when affliction or persecution ariseth for the word's sake, immediately they are offended.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFAnd these are they which are sown among thorns; such as hear the word,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”and the cares of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the lusts of other things entering in, choke the word, and it becometh unfruitful.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡And these are they which are sown on good ground; such as hear the word, and receive it, and bring forth fruit, some thirtyfold, some sixty, and some an hundred.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxAnd he said unto them, Is a candle brought to be put under a bushel, or under a bed? and not to be set on a candlestick?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{For there is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested; neither was any thing kept secret, but that it should come abroad.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ+If any man have ears to hear, let him hear.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’And he said unto them, Take heed what ye hear: with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you: and unto you that hear shall more be given.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoFor he that hath, to him shall be given: and he that hath not, from him shall be taken even that which he hath.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTAnd he said, So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙeand should sleep, and rise night and day, and the seed should spring and grow up, he knoweth not how.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙrFor the earth bringeth forth fruit of herself; first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgBut when the fruit is brought forth, immediately he putteth in the sickle, because the harvest is come.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd he said, Whereunto shall we liken the kingdom of God? or with what comparison shall we compare it?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyIt is like a grain of mustard seed, which, when it is sown in the earth, is less than all the seeds that be in the earth:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨but when it is sown, it groweth up, and becometh greater than all herbs, and shooteth out great branches; so that the fowls of the air may lodge under the shadow of it.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book(§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse"¦verses’„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVAnd with many such parables spake he the word unto them, as they were able to hear it.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙqBut without a parable spake he not unto them: and when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples.¦layout ªlast_verse"…¤book(§chapter«first_verse#ªlast_verse&¦verses”„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcAnd the same day, when the even was come, he saith unto them, Let us pass over unto the other side.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And when they had sent away the multitude, they took him even as he was in the ship. And there were also with him other little ships.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaAnd there arose a great storm of wind, and the waves beat into the ship, so that it was now full.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŒAnd he was in the hinder part of the ship, asleep on a pillow: and they awake him, and say unto him, Master, carest thou not that we perish?¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book(§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse)¦verses“„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|And he arose, and rebuked the wind, and said unto the sea, Peace, be still. And the wind ceased, and there was a great calm.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNAnd he said unto them, Why are ye so fearful? how is it that ye have no faith?¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzAnd they feared exceedingly, and said one to another, What manner of man is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?¦layout ªlast_verse)